---

<BlogLayout
  title="How to Buy Your Next Home Before Selling Your Current One | Cliffco Mortgage"
  description="Timing a home purchase around a sale is one of the hardest parts of the move-up process. Here's how bridge loans, contingent offers, and smart sequencing actually work."
  type="article"
>
  <Breadcrumbs trail={[
    { name: "Home", href: "/" },
    { name: "Blog", href: "/mortgage-guides/blog/" },
    { name: "Buy Before Selling Your Current Home", href: "/mortgage-guides/blog/how-to-buy-home-before-selling-current-one/" },
  ]} />

  <article class="container-prose pt-12 pb-16">

    <h1 style="font-size: var(--text-display-lg);">How to Buy Your Next Home Before Selling Your Current One</h1>

    <ArticleImage />

    <p class="mt-8">If you own a home and want to buy another one, you've already figured out the central problem: you need the proceeds from your sale to fund your purchase, but you need to know where you're going before you can commit to leaving where you are. Most move-up buyers try to solve that problem by timing both transactions to close simultaneously, and most of them discover that the market doesn't cooperate with that plan. This article is about how to approach the sequencing problem before it forces a bad decision on either end.</p>

    <h2 class="mt-10">Why Simultaneous Closings Are Harder Than They Sound</h2>

    <p class="mt-4">The logic of selling and buying on the same day is appealing. You're never without a home, you capture your equity exactly when you need it, and everything lines up cleanly. In practice, simultaneous closings require two separate transactions involving four parties, two sets of lenders, two title companies, and two sets of contingencies to all complete on the same day without a single delay. A problem on either side collapses the whole structure. Sellers who've accepted a contingent offer from you may find another buyer. Your purchase may fall through while you've already accepted an offer on your current home. The dependency between the two deals creates risk that experienced buyers learn to reduce rather than accept.</p>

    <h2 class="mt-10">The Hidden Cost of Getting Sequence Wrong</h2>

    <p class="mt-4">Here's what move-up buyers don't fully account for until they're in it. Selling first and then buying puts you in the strongest possible financial position but the weakest negotiating position. You're a motivated buyer with a defined timeline and potentially temporary housing costs adding pressure every week. Sellers and their agents can read that. Offers written under that kind of pressure tend to be less disciplined than they should be.</p>

    <p class="mt-4">Buying first and then selling puts you in the strongest negotiating position on the purchase but creates real financial exposure. Carrying two mortgages simultaneously, even for a short period, strains most households. If the sale of your current home takes longer than expected or comes in below your target price, that exposure compounds.</p>

    <h2 class="mt-10">The Options That Actually Solve the Problem</h2>

    <p class="mt-4"><strong>Bridge loans</strong> are short-term financing products that allow you to borrow against the equity in your current home to fund the purchase of your next one before the sale closes. The bridge loan is repaid when your current home sells. This approach lets you make a non-contingent offer on your next home, which is significantly more competitive than a contingent one, while still relying on your equity to fund the purchase. Bridge loans carry higher rates than conventional mortgages and have fees that belong in your cost comparison, but they eliminate the timing dependency between the two transactions.</p>

    <p class="mt-4"><strong>Contingent offers</strong> remain a viable path in slower markets or with sellers who have the flexibility to wait. A contingency protects you by making your purchase dependent on the successful sale of your current home. The tradeoff is that sellers in competitive markets often prefer non-contingent offers, and a contingent offer in a multiple-offer situation is frequently passed over regardless of price.</p>

    <p class="mt-4"><strong>Selling first with a leaseback</strong> is an underused option that solves the sequencing problem differently. You sell your current home, negotiate a leaseback period with the buyer that gives you 30 to 90 days to remain in the property, and use that window to find and close on your next home without carrying two mortgages. The leaseback keeps your equity liquid and your timeline controlled without the cost of a bridge loan.</p>

    <h2 class="mt-10">What to Do, In Order</h2>

    <ol class="mt-4 list-decimal list-inside space-y-2">
      <li><strong>Get pre-approved for your next purchase before you list your current home.</strong> Knowing your buying power without depending on the sale proceeds tells you what options are available to you and whether a bridge loan makes sense for your equity position.</li>
      <li><strong>Ask your lender to model the bridge loan scenario.</strong> Understand the cost of carrying the bridge, the rate, the fees, and the monthly payment on both properties during the overlap period. Compare that cost to the value of making a non-contingent offer in your target market.</li>
      <li><strong>Have your current home evaluated before you start shopping.</strong> A realistic market value estimate tells you what equity you're working with and what your net proceeds will be after commissions and closing costs. That number drives every other calculation in the move-up sequence.</li>
      <li><strong>Explore leaseback terms with your listing agent before you accept an offer on your current home.</strong> Not every buyer will agree to a leaseback, but many will, particularly in markets where sellers have leverage. Structuring it into the sale contract costs nothing to ask for.</li>
      <li><strong>Resist the pressure to over-offer on your next home because of timeline anxiety.</strong> Move-up buyers who are under pressure to close before a leaseback expires or before their bridge loan costs mount tend to make offers they wouldn't otherwise make. Know your ceiling before the pressure starts.</li>
    </ol>

    <!-- Embedded lite Payment Estimator -->
    <div class="mt-12 border border-[var(--color-accent)]/25 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden">
      <div class="bg-[var(--color-accent)]/8 px-6 py-5 border-b border-[var(--color-accent)]/15">
        <p class="eyebrow text-xs mb-1">Quick check</p>
        <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-[var(--color-ink)]">Estimate your monthly payment.</h2>
        <p class="text-sm text-[var(--color-text-muted)] mt-1">Enter the numbers for any property to see your estimated monthly payment before you make an offer.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="p-6">
        <div class="grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-3">
          <div>
            <label class="block text-sm font-medium text-[var(--color-ink)] mb-1">Home price ($)</label>
            <input id="art-pe-price" type="number" value="500000" min="50000" step="5000" class="art-calc-input" />
          </div>
          <div>
            <label class="block text-sm font-medium text-[var(--color-ink)] mb-1">Down payment (%)</label>
            <input id="art-pe-down" type="number" value="20" min="3" max="50" step="1" class="art-calc-input" />
          </div>
          <div>
            <label class="block text-sm font-medium text-[var(--color-ink)] mb-1">Interest rate (%)</label>
            <input id="art-pe-rate" type="number" value="7.0" min="1" max="15" step="0.125" class="art-calc-input" />
          </div>
          <div>
            <label class="block text-sm font-medium text-[var(--color-ink)] mb-1">Loan term</label>
            <select id="art-pe-term" class="art-calc-input">
              <option value="30">30-year fixed</option>
              <option value="20">20-year fixed</option>
              <option value="15">15-year fixed</option>
            </select>
          </div>
          <div>
            <label class="block text-sm font-medium text-[var(--color-ink)] mb-1">Annual taxes ($)</label>
            <input id="art-pe-taxes" type="number" value="6000" min="0" step="100" class="art-calc-input" />
          </div>
          <div>
            <label class="block text-sm font-medium text-[var(--color-ink)] mb-1">Annual insurance ($)</label>
            <input id="art-pe-ins" type="number" value="2400" min="0" step="100" class="art-calc-input" />
          </div>
        </div>
        <button id="art-pe-btn" class="btn btn-primary mt-5">Estimate Payment</button>

        <div id="art-pe-result" class="hidden mt-6">
          <div class="p-5 bg-[var(--color-mist-gray)] rounded-xl">
            <div class="grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-3">
              <div>
                <p class="text-xs text-[var(--color-text-muted)] uppercase tracking-wide">Principal &amp; Interest</p>
                <p id="art-pe-pi" class="text-3xl font-bold text-[var(--color-accent)] mt-1"></p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-xs text-[var(--color-text-muted)] uppercase tracking-wide">Taxes &amp; Insurance</p>
                <p id="art-pe-ti" class="text-2xl font-bold text-[var(--color-ink)] mt-1"></p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-xs text-[var(--color-text-muted)] uppercase tracking-wide">Total Monthly Payment</p>
                <p id="art-pe-total" class="text-2xl font-bold text-[var(--color-ink)] mt-1"></p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <p id="art-pe-note" class="mt-3 text-xs text-[var(--color-text-muted)]"></p>
          </div>

          <div class="mt-4 p-5 bg-[var(--color-rich-black)] rounded-xl flex flex-col sm:flex-row sm:items-center gap-4 justify-between">
            <div>
              <p class="text-white font-semibold">Know your number before you make an offer.</p>
              <p class="text-white/70 text-sm mt-1">A Cliffco loan officer can lock a rate and get you pre-approved the same day.</p>
            </div>
            <a href="/get-started/" class="btn btn-primary !bg-white !text-[var(--color-rich-black)] shrink-0 whitespace-nowrap">Talk to a loan officer</a>
          </div>

          <p class="mt-4 text-sm text-center text-[var(--color-text-muted)]">
            Want to model other scenarios? <a href="/mortgage-guides/calculators/payment-estimator/" class="text-[var(--color-accent)] font-medium">View the full payment estimator →</a>
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

  </article>
</BlogLayout>

<style>
  .art-calc-input {
    width: 100%;
    border: 1px solid var(--color-soft-gray, #d1d5db);
    border-radius: 0.5rem;
    padding: 0.5rem 0.75rem;
    font-size: 0.875rem;
    color: var(--color-ink);
    background: white;
    outline: none;
    transition: border-color 0.15s;
  }
  .art-calc-input:focus {
    border-color: var(--color-accent);
    box-shadow: 0 0 0 3px color-mix(in srgb, var(--color-accent) 15%, transparent);
  }
</style>

<script>
  const peBtn = document.getElementById("art-pe-btn")!;
  peBtn.addEventListener("click", () => {
    const price = +(document.getElementById("art-pe-price") as HTMLInputElement).value;
    const downPct = +(document.getElementById("art-pe-down") as HTMLInputElement).value;
    const rate = +(document.getElementById("art-pe-rate") as HTMLInputElement).value;
    const term = +(document.getElementById("art-pe-term") as HTMLSelectElement).value;
    const taxes = +(document.getElementById("art-pe-taxes") as HTMLInputElement).value;
    const ins = +(document.getElementById("art-pe-ins") as HTMLInputElement).value;

    const loanAmount = price * (1 - downPct / 100);
    const r = rate / 100 / 12;
    const n = term * 12;
    const pi = r === 0 ? loanAmount / n : loanAmount * (r * Math.pow(1 + r, n)) / (Math.pow(1 + r, n) - 1);
    const ti = (taxes + ins) / 12;
    const total = pi + ti;
    const fmt = (v: number) => "$" + Math.round(v).toLocaleString("en-US");

    (document.getElementById("art-pe-pi") as HTMLElement).textContent = fmt(pi);
    (document.getElementById("art-pe-ti") as HTMLElement).textContent = fmt(ti);
    (document.getElementById("art-pe-total") as HTMLElement).textContent = fmt(total);
    (document.getElementById("art-pe-note") as HTMLElement).textContent =
      `Loan amount: ${fmt(loanAmount)} | Down payment: ${fmt(price * downPct / 100)}. Estimate only - does not include PMI, HOA, or other costs.`;
    (document.getElementById("art-pe-result") as HTMLElement).classList.remove("hidden");
  });
</script>
